  { bg: "Arts, science or non-IT graduates", door: "Hardest start. Aim for an ordinary job (finance, stores, HR) in a company that runs SAP, then move toward configuration. Ask about SAP Learning's free student edition if you are enrolled.", warn: "Paying an institute for a promised job is the expensive wrong turn here." },
  { bg: "Working professional in finance, supply or HR", door: "The strongest route. Your process knowledge is the scarce part. Become the SAP point person on your team, then the module owner, then consult.", warn: "Switching to a fresher support role first can cost a large pay cut. Compare before you move." },
];

const credRows = [
  { thing: "SAP's own certification", what: "A performance-based exam through SAP Learning, with a short annual assessment to stay current. SAP says preparation runs through a Learning Hub subscription.", worth: "A real, checkable credential. Useful as a floor, not as proof you can do the job." },
  { thing: "A certificate from a training institute", what: "A paper from the institute. It is the institute's own document, not SAP's.", worth: "Adds nothing a recruiter can verify with SAP. Judge the institute by what you could build afterwards." },
  { thing: "Free SAP learning content", what: "Former openSAP courses now sit inside SAP Learning, with free self-paced journeys. A free S/4HANA Cloud trial also exists, but its length differs by SAP region.", worth: "Enough to test whether you like the work before spending anything." },
  { thing: "Free student edition", what: "For students enrolled in a university: full course catalogue, live sessions, access to cloud practice systems and two certification attempts, at no cost.", worth: "If you are enrolled, use this before paying anyone." },
];

const instituteQuestions = [
  { num: "1", title: "Is the institute an official SAP training provider?", body: "Ask for proof you can check with SAP Education in India. SAP Community threads over the years describe look-alike institutes that promise jobs and run on pirated software. Treat that as a warning, not a statistic." },
  { num: "2", title: "What will you have built when the course ends?", body: "A good answer names a process you ran end to end in a practice system: for FICO, a vendor invoice from posting to payment; for MM, a purchase requisition to goods receipt. A syllabus list is not an answer." },
  { num: "3", title: "Does the fee come with exam attempts and a real system?", body: "Official exam fees are listed on SAP's own certification shop, so check the current fee there before you pay anyone. Set that fee against the PayScale entry average of about Rs 3.65 lakh a year, and ask what an institute adds to it." },
  { num: "4", title: "Does it promise a job or a placement?", body: "Nobody can promise an SAP job. Hiring managers decide, and fresher seats are limited. A promise is a sales line." },
  { num: "5", title: "Could you get the same content free first?", body: "Yes, for the basics. Sample a free learning journey and the trial system first. Pay only for structure, feedback or a system you cannot get free." },
];

const payRows = [
  { stage: "SAP consultant, under 1 year", figure: "About Rs 3.65 lakh a year, total pay", base: "160 salaries", src: "PayScale India SAP Consultant page, updated 23 May 2026" },
  { stage: "SAP consultant, 1 to 4 years", figure: "About Rs 5.99 lakh a year, total pay", base: "591 salaries", src: "Same page. That is about 64% above the first-year average." },
  { stage: "SAP consultant, all experience", figure: "Average base Rs 7.98 lakh; the middle 80% sit between about Rs 3.48 lakh and Rs 20 lakh", base: "960 profiles", src: "Same page" },
  { stage: "Senior SAP consultant, 5 to 9 years", figure: "About Rs 13.3 lakh a year, total pay", base: "187 salaries", src: "PayScale India Senior SAP Consultant page, updated 4 July 2026" },
