  <h2 id="work-reality">Shifts, travel and burnout</h2>

  <div class="week-grid">
    <div class="week-card">
      <div class="week-card__who">Project phase</div>
      <p>Workshops and testing are heavy. Cutover to a new system often means nights or weekends, followed by a period of extra support after go-live. Practitioner writing describes this pattern; no survey measures it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="week-card">
      <div class="week-card__who">Support and AMS</div>
      <p>Ticket queues, month-end peaks and shift rotation are common. An old SAP Community thread and an old employee review say night and weekend duty can be long; both are dated and anecdotal. Ask any employer for the shift pattern in writing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="week-card">
      <div class="week-card__who">Travel</div>
      <p>Client-site stints can be part of consulting, and some employers allow remote or hybrid work. How much depends on the firm and the client, so ask before you sign.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The practical point: the easiest-to-enter roles carry the least control over your hours. Planning the move from support to build or configuration is also a work-life move.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">What AI changes in SAP work</h2>

  <p>
    SAP is building AI into its own tools. Its Joule assistant for consultants reads and explains custom ABAP code, answers from SAP documentation and gives configuration guidance. SAP's page claims up to 40% less time interpreting custom code, up to 50% less redesign effort and up to 14% faster delivery, but these are "up to" figures, and the page we could read does not show the basis.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Pressure</th>
          <th>What is being automated</th>
          <th>What still needs a person</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Pressure"><strong>Reading and writing simple code</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is being automated">Code explanation, simple code generation and unit-test drafts.</td>
          <td data-label="What still needs a person">Knowing which change is safe in this client's landscape.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Pressure"><strong>Looking things up</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is being automated">Answers from SAP documentation and guided configuration steps.</td>
          <td data-label="What still needs a person">Choosing the right process for this business and getting people to adopt it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Pressure"><strong>Entry-level tasks</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is being automated">Routine support answers and first-draft documents.</td>
          <td data-label="What still needs a person">Judgement on odd cases, signing off, owning a go-live.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Staged path: now, use AI to explain code and draft documents while you check every answer. Next, learn to build with APIs and extensions on BTP. Later, move toward design and ownership work that tools assist but do not own. Do not take "10x" claims as a promise.
  </p>

  <h2 id="grow">Who pays, and how they trust you</h2>

  <p>
    In employed roles, the services firm or the company pays you. In consulting, a client's finance, supply or IT head pays, and trust comes from references, not adverts.
  </p>
  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">The 3 Gates, applied to SAP</p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate"><span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span><span class="gate__name">Proof of Skill</span><p>One process run end to end in a practice system, written up in plain words.</p></div>
      <div class="gate"><span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span><span class="gate__name">Proof of Communication</span><p>A two-minute spoken explanation a non-IT colleague can follow.</p></div>
      <div class="gate"><span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span><span class="gate__name">Proof of Value</span><p>Three people from the business side who say the fix mattered to them.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first sales skill worth learning is positioning: say in one line which module, which industry and which problem you fix. The second is asking for referrals right after a go-live. One number to watch: how many conversations turn into a second piece of work. For most routes here the sales layer matters mainly for the later independent step.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Building a high-value skill portfolio here means one domain plus one multiplier</strong>, such as finance plus BTP, or supply plus analytics, with visible proof. That helps you make the decisions that unlock high income opportunities earlier. Show the proof on one shelf: answer questions on SAP Community or post one plain process note on LinkedIn, and let people you have worked with refer you.
  </p>

